# Package Diagram Reference
 
## Purpose
The Package Diagram groups elements (classes, components, other packages) into packages. It shows the dependencies between the packages.
 
## Inputs
- A list of the classes, components, or modules.
- The rule that you use to group them (for example, by feature or by layer).
- The dependencies between the groups.
## Symbols
| Symbol | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Folder shape (rectangle with a small tab) | A package |
| Dashed arrow | A dependency |
| Dashed arrow with `<<import>>` | The target elements become visible in the source package |
| Dashed arrow with `<<access>>` | The source package uses the target, but does not merge names |
 
## Procedure
1. Choose one grouping rule. Use that rule for the entire diagram.
2. Create one package for each group.
3. Write a clear name on each package.
4. Draw a dependency arrow from a package to each package that it uses.
5. Look for circular dependencies. Remove them.
6. Show nested packages only if you need them.
## Rules
- Make each package cohesive. Its contents must be related.
- Make coupling between packages low.
- Do not make circular dependencies.
- Draw dependency arrows in one main direction (for example, from top to bottom).
- Use the same grouping rule at each level.
---
 
## Final check
 
Do this check before you give the output to the user.
 
Do this check before you give the output to the user.
 
1. Make sure that the diagram has a title and a diagram type.
2. Make sure that all symbols are standard, or that a legend explains them.
3. Make sure that names are clear and consistent.
4. Make sure that the diagram follows the Rules section of this skill.
5. Make sure that the level of detail is correct for the audience.
6. Make sure that the text follows the STE writing rules.
7. If you gave diagram code, make sure that the syntax is valid for the tool.